/**
 * ONE LINE, ABOVE THE FOLD, ON THE PAGES THAT PRINT A NUMBER SOMEBODY COULD TRADE ON.
 *
 * The site already had a strong disclaimer. It was on /terms, and /terms was linked from
 * exactly one page — /about. The footer said "Not investment advice" as plain text, linking
 * nowhere, and the footer list had no Terms entry at all. So the strongest thing written here
 * was unreachable from the four calculators and the liquidation map, which are the only pages
 * where it does any work.
 *
 * That is the whole finding: a disclaimer nobody can reach from the risky page is decoration.
 * This makes it operative, in one muted line that a reader can follow, and the same line
 * appears on every page of that kind rather than being reworded per page.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT A WARNING BOX. A yellow panel above a calculator reads as a formality and
 * gets skipped; it also costs the page its first impression. This is small, quiet, adjacent to
 * the lede, and it links — which is the only property that actually matters.
 */
---
<p class="advisory">
  Not investment advice — modelled figures from stated assumptions.
  {" "}<a href="/terms">Terms and disclaimer</a>
</p>
